   <!-- Privacy Sandbox -->
   <message name="IDS_SETTINGS_PRIVACY_SANDBOX_LEARN_MORE_DIALOG_TOPICS_TITLE" desc="TOPICS API A sub title that describes the first of 2 ways a site can get info from Chrome in order to target ads to the user. Topics of interest include things like ΓÇ£Live comedyΓÇ¥ and ΓÇ£Rock musicΓÇ¥. Chrome estimates these interests based on the sites users visit. * ΓÇ£YourΓÇ¥: we need to convey a sense of ownership, even if the user didnΓÇÖt explicitly choose the interests weΓÇÖre talking about. Without the ΓÇ£yourΓÇ¥, it could sound like Chrome is simply building a generic library of interests. * ΓÇ£estimatedΓÇ¥: ItΓÇÖs an ΓÇ£estimationΓÇ¥, and we donΓÇÖt want to suggest that we know with certainty the userΓÇÖs interests. Avoid words like ΓÇ£guessΓÇ¥, ΓÇ£establishΓÇ¥, ΓÇ£defineΓÇ¥, etc. (in place of ΓÇ£estimateΓÇ¥). * ΓÇ£by ChromeΓÇ¥: This is new behavior for a browser (Chrome) to take an active role in processing user data for the purposes of showing ads. ItΓÇÖs important to convey ΓÇ£chromeΓÇ¥, the actor in this case.">
-    Your interests as estimated by Chromium
+    Your interests as estimated by VELOX
   </message>
   <message name="IDS_SETTINGS_PRIVACY_SANDBOX_LEARN_MORE_DIALOG_TOPICS_DATA_TYPES" desc="TOPICS API Bullet 1 of 3 that appear beneath the ΓÇ£Your interests as estimated by ChromeΓÇ¥ subtitle. We break the description into the 3 pieces users most care about: 1) what data, 2) why this data, 3) what can I do about it? * ΓÇ£What data is used:ΓÇ¥ is bold and serves as a label to help people skimming the page. * ΓÇ£Your browsing historyΓÇ¥: The userΓÇÖs browsing history is available beneath the ΓÇ£HistoryΓÇ¥ menu at ChromeΓÇÖs top level. The Topics API estimates topics of interest based on sites in the userΓÇÖs browsing history that are participating in the trials.">
-    Your browsing history, a record of sites you've visited using Chromium on this device.
+    Your browsing history, a record of sites you've visited using VELOX on this device.
   </message>
   <message name="IDS_SETTINGS_PRIVACY_SANDBOX_LEARN_MORE_DIALOG_TOPICS_DATA_USAGE" desc="TOPICS API Bullet 2 of 3 that appear beneath the ΓÇ£Your interests as estimated by ChromeΓÇ¥ subtitle. We break the description into the 3 pieces users most care about: 1) what data, 2) why this data, 3) what can I do about it? * ΓÇ£How we use this data:ΓÇ¥ is bold and serves as a label to help people skimming the page. * ΓÇ£estimate your interestsΓÇ¥: Topics of interest include things like ΓÇ£Live comedyΓÇ¥ and ΓÇ£Rock musicΓÇ¥. Chrome estimates these interests based on the sites users visit. ItΓÇÖs an ΓÇ£estimationΓÇ¥, and we donΓÇÖt want to suggest that we know with certainty the userΓÇÖs interests. Avoid words like ΓÇ£guessΓÇ¥, ΓÇ£establishΓÇ¥, ΓÇ£defineΓÇ¥, etc. (in place of ΓÇ£estimateΓÇ¥).">
-    Chromium can estimate your interests. Later, a site you visit can ask Chromium to see your interests in order to personalize the ads you see.
+    VELOX can estimate your interests. Later, a site you visit can ask VELOX to see your interests in order to personalize the ads you see.
   </message>
   <message name="IDS_SETTINGS_PRIVACY_SANDBOX_LEARN_MORE_DIALOG_TOPICS_DATA_MANAGEMENT" desc="TOPICS API Bullet 3 of 3 that appear beneath the ΓÇ£Your interests as estimated by ChromeΓÇ¥ subtitle. We break the description into the 3 pieces users most care about: 1) what data, 2) why this data, 3) what can I do about it? * ΓÇ£How you can manage your data:ΓÇ¥ is bold and serves as a label to help people skimming the page. * ΓÇ£To protect your privacyΓÇ¥: Today, most data is kept for long periods of time. This new Topics API doesnΓÇÖt retain estimated topics for more than 3 weeks. The only reason for this feature is to protect the userΓÇÖs privacy. * ΓÇ£autoΓÇ¥: isnΓÇÖt strictly necessary, but it conveys that this feature is an ongoing part of the service. This isnΓÇÖt describing, for example, a one-time deletion. * ΓÇ£older than 4 weeksΓÇ¥: WeΓÇÖre trying to convey a rolling delete. Imagine a user opens a browser for the very first time. As the user browses, Chrome will estimate up to 5 topics of interest in the first week. In the second week, Chrome will estimate up to 5 additional topics of interest. The same for the third week, for a total of 15 potential interests. In week 4, Chrome will automatically delete the 5 interests from week 1 so that there arenΓÇÖt ever more than 15 topics associated with a user at any given time. * ΓÇ£interests can refreshΓÇ¥: If a user loves horror films and reads about them for months on end, the interest ΓÇ£Horror moviesΓÇ¥ might get deleted and refreshed in the same week, so that ΓÇ£Horror moviesΓÇ¥ doesnΓÇÖt ever disappear from the userΓÇÖs list of interests. * ΓÇ£Or you canΓÇªΓÇ¥: A user might love horror movies but canΓÇÖt stand the idea of seeing ads about horror movies (because even the visuals will ruin the surprise of opening night). In this case, the user can remove ΓÇ£Horror moviesΓÇ¥ from the list of interests Chrome will consider. This removal remains in place indefinitely. Note that the user might still see ads about Horror movies from different ad networks or by other means than ChromeΓÇÖs ΓÇ£Browser-based ad personalizationΓÇ¥ setting.">
-    To protect your privacy, we auto-delete your interests that are older than 4 weeks. As you keep browsing, an interest might appear on the list again. Or you can remove interests you don't want Chromium to consider.
+    To protect your privacy, we auto-delete your interests that are older than 4 weeks. As you keep browsing, an interest might appear on the list again. Or you can remove interests you don't want VELOX to consider.
   </message>
   <message name="IDS_SETTINGS_PRIVACY_SANDBOX_LEARN_MORE_DIALOG_FLEDGE_DATA_TYPES" desc="FLEDGE API Bullet 1 of 3 that appear beneath the ΓÇ£Sites you visit that define your interestsΓÇ¥ subtitle. We break the description into the 3 pieces users most care about: 1) what data, 2) why this data, 3) what can I do about it? * ΓÇ£What data is used:ΓÇ¥ is bold and serves as a label to help people skimming the page. * ΓÇ£Your browsing historyΓÇ¥: The userΓÇÖs browsing history is available beneath the ΓÇ£HistoryΓÇ¥ menu at ChromeΓÇÖs top level. This content taken in isolation is somewhat misleading. With FLEDGE, a site can store information in Chrome about the userΓÇÖs visit to their site. For example, the site might store ΓÇ£running_shoes_red_size_35ΓÇ¥ to indicate that the user showed interest in a particular shoe. WeΓÇÖre OK with the simplification in this bullet because 1) we explain it in the next bullet and 2) we want the user to understand that at the top level, both Topics &amp; Fledge are based on the same thing and that is the userΓÇÖs browsing history.">
-    Your browsing history, a record of sites you've visited using Chromium on this device.
+    Your browsing history, a record of sites you've visited using VELOX on this device.
   </message>
   <message name="IDS_SETTINGS_PRIVACY_SANDBOX_LEARN_MORE_DIALOG_FLEDGE_DATA_USAGE" desc="FLEDGE API Bullet 2 of 3 that appear beneath the ΓÇ£Sites you visit that define your interestsΓÇ¥ subtitle. We break the description into the 3 pieces users most care about: 1) what data, 2) why this data, 3) what can I do about it? * ΓÇ£How we use this data:ΓÇ¥ is bold and serves as a label to help people skimming the page. With FLEDGE, a site can store information in Chrome about the userΓÇÖs visit to their site. For example, the site might store ΓÇ£running_shoes_red_size_35ΓÇ¥ to indicate that the user showed interest in a particular shoe. WeΓÇÖre OK with the simplification in this bullet because 1) we explain it in the next bullet and 2) we want the user to understand that at the top level, both Topics &amp; Fledge are based on the same thing and that is the userΓÇÖs browsing history. * ΓÇ£LaterΓÇ¥:  is important to convey the passage of time. We donΓÇÖt want to convey how much time (it could be tiny). WhatΓÇÖs important is that the user understands this is essentially a two-step process. They browse, and Chrome might estimate their interests as they do so. At a later point when they continue to visit a site, thatΓÇÖs when the estimated interests might come into play.">
-    Sites can store information with Chromium about your interests. For example, if you visit a site to buy shoes for a marathon, the site might define your interest as running marathons. Later, if you visit a different site to register for a race, that site can show you an ad for running shoes based on your interests.
+    Sites can store information with VELOX about your interests. For example, if you visit a site to buy shoes for a marathon, the site might define your interest as running marathons. Later, if you visit a different site to register for a race, that site can show you an ad for running shoes based on your interests.
   </message>
   <message name="IDS_SETTINGS_PRIVACY_SANDBOX_AD_PERSONALIZATION_DIALOG_DESCRIPTION" desc="A paragraph beneath the card title. Note that this paragraph is written so that it describes both the Topics API and the FLEDGE API. Some things are described slightly differently for this reason. Version 1 of 3 of this paragraph.  1) The first line is written with the assumption that the user a) has Privacy Sandbox trials turned on and b) has been randomly selected by Chrome to be in  an active experiment. As a result of both conditions, this user should have data on this page (a list of interests and a list of sites). 2) NOT THE CASE FOR THIS STRING: A different version of  this first line is written for the case that a user has the Privacy Sandbox trials turned on but they havenΓÇÖt been randomly selected to be in a trial. 3) NOT THE CASE FOR THIS STRING: A different version of  this first line is written for the case that a user doesnΓÇÖt have the Privacy Sandbox trials turned on. * ΓÇ£browsing historyΓÇ¥:  ΓÇ£a record of sites youΓÇÖve visited using Chrome on this device.ΓÇ¥ * ΓÇ£affects the ads you see and the interests as estimated belowΓÇ¥: browsing history leads to Chrome establishing user interests leads to 1) the ads you see are different, and 2) the list of things on this page changes based on what Chrome establishes.* ΓÇ£To protect your privacyΓÇ¥: Today, most data is kept for long periods of time. This new Topics API doesnΓÇÖt retain estimated topics for more than 3 weeks. The only reason for this feature is to protect the userΓÇÖs privacy. * ΓÇ£autoΓÇ¥: isnΓÇÖt strictly necessary, but it conveys that this feature is an ongoing part of the service. This isnΓÇÖt describing, for example, a one-time deletion. * ΓÇ£rolling basis each monthΓÇ¥: Information associated with Topics and FLEDGE are deleted on a rolling basis at least once per month. ItΓÇÖs simpler to talk about ΓÇ£once per monthΓÇ¥ rather than 3 weeks for Topics and 4 weeks for FLEDGE. * ΓÇ£Interests can refreshΓÇªΓÇ¥: We intend for this line to cover both Topics and FLEDGE. ItΓÇÖs slightly messy, because there is a suggestion that this line is more about Topics than FLEDGE (ΓÇ£interestsΓÇ¥ is more prominent in the Topics subtitle).">
-    Your browsing history affects the ads you see and the interests as estimated below. To protect your privacy, Chromium auto-deletes your interests on a rolling basis each month. Interests can refresh unless you remove them.
+    Your browsing history affects the ads you see and the interests as estimated below. To protect your privacy, VELOX auto-deletes your interests on a rolling basis each month. Interests can refresh unless you remove them.
   </message>
   <message name="IDS_SETTINGS_PRIVACY_SANDBOX_AD_PERSONALIZATION_DIALOG_DESCRIPTION_TRIALS_OFF" desc="A paragraph beneath the card title. Note that this paragraph is written so that it describes both the Topics API and the FLEDGE API. Some things are described slightly differently for this reason. Version 2 of 3 of this paragraph.  1) NOT THE CASE FOR THIS STRING: A different version of this first line is written for the case that a user has Privacy Sandbox trials turned on AND that theyΓÇÖve been randomly selected for a trial. 2) The first line is written with the assumption that the user 1) has Privacy Sandbox trials turned on but 2) has NOT been randomly selected by Chrome to be in  an active experiment. This explains why the user doesnΓÇÖt have data shown on the page. 3) NOT THE CASE FOR THIS STRING: A different version of  this first line is written for the case that a user doesnΓÇÖt have the Privacy Sandbox trials turned on. * ΓÇ£browsing historyΓÇ¥:  ΓÇ£a record of sites youΓÇÖve visited using Chrome on this device.ΓÇ¥ * ΓÇ£affects the ads you see and the interests as estimated belowΓÇ¥: browsing history leads to Chrome establishing user interests leads to 1) the ads you see are different, and 2) the list of things on this page changes based on what Chrome establishes. * ΓÇ£To protect your privacyΓÇ¥: Today, most data is kept for long periods of time. This new Topics API doesnΓÇÖt retain estimated topics for more than 3 weeks. The only reason for this feature is to protect the userΓÇÖs privacy. * ΓÇ£autoΓÇ¥: isnΓÇÖt strictly necessary, but it conveys that this feature is an ongoing part of the service. This isnΓÇÖt describing, for example, a one-time deletion. * ΓÇ£rolling basis each monthΓÇ¥: Information associated with Topics and FLEDGE are deleted on a rolling basis at least once per month. ItΓÇÖs simpler to talk about ΓÇ£once per monthΓÇ¥ rather than 3 weeks for Topics and 4 weeks for FLEDGE.* ΓÇ£Interests can refreshΓÇªΓÇ¥: We intend for this line to cover both Topics and FLEDGE. ItΓÇÖs slightly messy, because there is a suggestion that this line is more about Topics than FLEDGE (ΓÇ£interestsΓÇ¥ is more prominent in the Topics subtitle).">
-    When trials are on and if Chromium has randomly placed you in an active trial, your browsing history affects the ads you see and the interests as estimated below. To protect your privacy, Chromium deletes your interests on a rolling basis each month.
+    When trials are on and if VELOX has randomly placed you in an active trial, your browsing history affects the ads you see and the interests as estimated below. To protect your privacy, VELOX deletes your interests on a rolling basis each month.
   </message>
   <message name="IDS_SETTINGS_PRIVACY_SANDBOX_AD_PERSONALIZATION_DIALOG_DESCRIPTION_LISTS_EMPTY" desc="A paragraph beneath the card title. Note that this paragraph is written so that it describes both the Topics API and the FLEDGE API. Some things are described slightly differently for this reason. Version 3 of 3 of this paragraph.  1) NOT THE CASE FOR THIS STRING: A different version of this first line is written for the case that a user has Privacy Sandbox trials turned on AND that theyΓÇÖve been randomly selected for a trial. 2) NOT THE CASE FOR THIS STRING: A different version of  this first line is written for the case that a user has the Privacy Sandbox trials turned on but they havenΓÇÖt been randomly selected to be in a trial. 3) The first line is written with the assumption that the user a) doesnΓÇÖt have Privacy Sandbox trials turned on. b) Because of this, they canΓÇÖt be randomly selected for a trial. This explains why the user doesnΓÇÖt have data shown on the page. * ΓÇ£browsing historyΓÇ¥:  ΓÇ£a record of sites youΓÇÖve visited using Chrome on this device.ΓÇ¥ * ΓÇ£affects the ads you see and the interests as estimated belowΓÇ¥: browsing history leads to Chrome establishing user interests leads to 1) the ads you see are different, and 2) the list of things on this page changes based on what Chrome establishes. * ΓÇ£To protect your privacyΓÇ¥: Today, most data is kept for long periods of time. This new Topics API doesnΓÇÖt retain estimated topics for more than 3 weeks. The only reason for this feature is to protect the userΓÇÖs privacy. * ΓÇ£autoΓÇ¥: isnΓÇÖt strictly necessary, but it conveys that this feature is an ongoing part of the service. This isnΓÇÖt describing, for example, a one-time deletion. * ΓÇ£rolling basis each monthΓÇ¥: Information associated with Topics and FLEDGE are deleted on a rolling basis at least once per month. ItΓÇÖs simpler to talk about ΓÇ£once per monthΓÇ¥ rather than 3 weeks for Topics and 4 weeks for FLEDGE. * ΓÇ£Interests can refreshΓÇªΓÇ¥: We intend for this line to cover both Topics and FLEDGE. ItΓÇÖs slightly messy, because there is a suggestion that this line is more about Topics than FLEDGE (ΓÇ£interestsΓÇ¥ is more prominent in the Topics subtitle).">
